For $t>0$ and $x\in\R^d$, the Poisson kernel $P(t,x)$ is defined by
$$
    P(t,x) = {2t // \omega_{d+1} (|x|^2 + t^2)^{(d+1)/2}},
$$
where $ \omega_d = {2\pi^{d/2} // \Gamma(d/2)} $ is the area of $d$ dimensional sphere.

We first calculate $||P(t,\cdot)||_p$ and $||\diff_tP(t,\cdot)||_p$, respectively.
For the first one, observe that $P(t,x) = t^{-d}P(1,t^{-1}x)$.
Thus
$$
    \int_{\R^d}P^p(t,x)dx = t^{-dp}\int_{\R^d}P^p(1,t^{-1}x)dx
    = t^{-d(p-1)}\int_{\R^d}P^p(1,x)dx.
$$
Especially, if $p=1$, by using the polar coordinate,
$$
    \int_{\R^d} P(1, x) dx = {2\omega_d // \omega_{d+1}}
    \int_0^oo {r^{d-1} //(r^2 + 1)^{(d+1)/2}}dr
    = {2\Gamma((d+1)/2) // \Gamma(d/2)\sqrt{\pi}}
    \int_0^oo {r^{d-1} //(r^2 + 1)^{(d+1)/2}}dr.
$$
By changing the variable $s = r^2/(r^2+1)$ (then $r^2 = s/(1-s)$) and recall the
following formula
$$
    \int_0^1 t^{a-1}(1-t)^{b-1}dt = {\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b) // \Gamma(a+b)},
$$
we have
$$
    \int_0^oo {r^{d-1} // (r^2+1)^{(d+1)/2}}dr
    = {1//2}\int_0^1 s^{(d-2)/2}(1-s)^{-1/2}ds
    = {\Gamma(d/2)\sqrt{\pi} // 2\Gamma((d+1)/2)},\quad
    \int_{\R^d}P(1, x)dx = 1.
$$
Thus for $1<=p<oo$, we obtain $||P(t,\cdot)||_p = N(d,p)t^{-d(p-1)/p}$, where
$N(d,1) = 1$. In addition, it is easy to show that $||P(t,\cdot)||_oo =
N(d)t^{-d}$.

On the other hand, one can easily find that
$$
    \diff_tP(t, x) = t^{-1}P(t, x) - {2(d+1)t^2 //
    \omega_{d+1}(|x|^2+t^2)^{(d+3)/2}},\quad
    \diff_tP(t,x) = t^{-d-1}Q(t^{-1}x),
$$
where
$$
    Q(x) = P(1,x) - {2(d+1) // \omega_{d+1}(|x|^2+1)^{(d+3)/2}}.
$$
Therefore, for $1<=p<oo$, we have $||\diff_tP(t,\cdot)||_p =
N(d,p)t^{-(p-1)(d+1)/p-p^{-1}}$, and $||\diff_tP(t,\cdot)||_oo = N(d)t^{-d-1}$ by
letting $p->oo$.

It is known that (see, for instance, \cite{PDE_Folland}) for any $g\in
L_p(\R^d)$, where $1<=p<=oo$, $u(x) = g*P(x^1,\cdot)(x')$ is well-defined on
$\R_+^{d+1}$ and is harmonic there. If $p<oo$, then $u(x^1,\cdot) -> g$ in $L_p$
as $x^1\downarrow 0$. 

In addition, let $1<=p,q,r<=oo$ such that $1+r^{-1} = p^{-1}+q^{-1}$. Then by
the Young's inequality, for $x^1>0$,
$$
    ||u(x^1,\cdot)||_r <= ||g||_p ||P(x^1,\cdot)||_q = N_1(d,q)
    (x^1)^{-d(q-1)/q} ||g||_p \quad ({oo // oo} \defeq 1);
$$
$$
    ||u_{x^1}(x^1,\cdot)||_r <= ||g||_p ||P_{x^1}(x^1,\cdot)||_q
    = N_2(d,q)(x^1)^{-(q-1)(d+1)/q-q^{-1}}||g||_p \quad ({oo//oo} \defeq 1,\
    {1//oo} \defeq 0),
$$
where $N_1(d,1) = 1$. Especially, if $q = 1$,
$$
    ||u(x^1,\cdot)||_p <= ||g||_p,\quad
    ||u_{x^1}(x^1,\cdot)||_p <= N(d)(x^1)^{-1}||g||_p.
$$

